          "prompt": "Explain how src/inventory/reorder.py decides what to order.",
          "answer": "Based on `src/inventory/reorder.py`, here's how the reorder policy works:\n\n## Two functions determine what and how much to order:\n\n### 1. `reorder_due()` \u2014 Which SKUs need reordering\n- Returns every SKU where current stock level is **at or below** `REORDER_THRESHOLD` (12)\n- Iterates through all items via `all_items()`, checks each with `level_for(item[\"sku\"])`\n- If `level_for(sku) <= 12`, that SKU is included in the reorder list\n\n### 2. `order_size(sku)` \u2014 How many units to order\n- **Formula**: `shortfall = REORDER_THRESHOLD * 3 - level_for(sku)`\n  - Target stock level is 3\u00d7 the threshold = 36 units\n  - `shortfall` = how far below 36 the current stock is\n- **Capping**: `min(max(shortfall, 0), MAX_BATCH)` \n  - `max(shortfall, 0)` ensures no negative order quantities\n  - `MAX_BATCH` (250) caps the maximum order size per run\n\n## In practice:\n- Items at \u226412 units trigger a reorder recommendation\n- Order quantity scales with how depleted the item is (up to 36-unit target)\n- No single order exceeds 250 units, even if an item is deeply stocked below target",
